----------\nMethod 180.1: Determination of Turbidity by Nephelometry (https://www.epa.gov/sites/default/files/2015-08/documents/method_180-1_1993.pdf)\nciteturn4view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 11; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.epa.gov/sites/default/files/2015-08/documents/method_180-1_1993.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 310\nL0@P0: www.epa.gov August 1993\nL1@P0: Method 180.1: Determination of\nL2@P0-1: Turbidity by NephelometryMETHOD 180.1\nL3@P1: DETERMINATION OF TURBIDITY BY NEPHELOMETRY\nL4@P1: Edited by James W. O'Dell\nL5@P1: Inorganic Chemistry Branch\nL6@P1: Chemistry Research Division\nL7@P1: Revision 2.0\nL8@P1: August 1993\nL9@P1: ENVIRONMENTAL MONITORING SYSTEMS LABORATORY\nL10@P1: OFFICE OF RESEARCH AND DEVELOPMENT\nL11@P1: U.S. ENVIRONMENTAL PROTECTION AGENCY\nL12@P1: CINCINNATI, OHIO 45268\nL13@P1-2: 180.1-1METHOD 180.1\nL14@P2: DETERMINATION OF TURBIDITY BY NEPHELOMETRY\nL15@P2: 1.0 SCOPE AND APPLICATION\nL16@P2: 1.1 This method covers the determination of turbidity in drinking, ground, surface,\nL17@P2: and saline waters, domestic and industrial wastes.\nL18@P2: 1.2 The applicable range is 0-40 nephelometric turbidity units (NTU). Higher\nL19@P2: values may be obtained with dilution of the sample.\nL20@P2: 2.0 SUMMARY OF METHOD\nL21@P2: 2.1 The method is based upon a comparison of the intensity of light scattered by\nL22@P2: the sample under defined conditions with the intensity of light scattered by a\nL23@P2: standard reference suspension. The higher the intensity of scattered light, the\nL24@P2: higher the turbidity. Readings, in NTU's, are made in a nephelometer\nL25@P2: designed according to specifications given in Sections 6.1 and 6.2. A primary\nL26@P2: standard suspension is used to calibrate the instrument. A secondary standard\nL27@P2: suspension is used as a daily calibration check and is monitored periodically\nL28@P2: for deterioration using one of the primary standards.\nL29@P2: 2.1.1 Formazin polymer is used as a primary turbidity suspension for water\nL30@P2: because it is more reproducible than other types of standards\nL31@P2: previously used for turbidity analysis.\nL32@P2: 2.1.2 A commercially available polymer primary standard is also approved\nL33@P2: for use for the National Interim Primary Drinking Water Regulations.\nL34@P2: This standard is identified as AMCO-AEPA-1, available from Advanced\nL35@P2: Polymer Systems.\nL36@P2: 3.0 DEFINITIONS\nL37@P2: 3.1 Calibration Blank (CB) -- A volume of reagent water fortified with the same\nL38@P2: matrix as the calibration standards, but without the analytes, internal\nL39@P2: standards, or surrogates analytes.\nL40@P2: 3.2 Instrument Performance Check Solution (IPC) -- A solution of one or more\nL41@P2: method analytes, surrogates, internal standards, or other test substances used\nL42@P2: to evaluate the performance of the instrument system with respect to a defined\nL43@P2: set of criteria.\nL44@P2: 3.3 Laboratory Reagent Blank (LRB) -- An aliquot of reagent water or other blank\nL45@P2: matrices that are treated exactly as a sample including exposure to all\nL46@P2: glassware, equipment, solvents, reagents, internal standards, and surrogates\nL47@P2: that are used with other samples. The LRB is used to determine if method\nL48@P2-3: 180.1-2180.1-3\nL49@P3: analytes or other interferences are present in the laboratory environment, the\nL50@P3: reagents, or the apparatus.\nL51@P3: 3.4 Linear Calibration Range (LCR) -- The concentration range over which the\nL52@P3: instrument response is linear.\nL53@P3: 3.5 Material Safety Data Sheet (MSDS) -- Written information provided by\nL54@P3: vendors concerning a chemical's toxicity, health hazards, physical properties,\nL55@P3: fire, and reactivity data including storage, spill, and handling precautions.\nL56@P3: 3.6 Primary Calibration Standard (PCAL) -- A suspension prepared from the\nL57@P3: primary dilution stock standard suspension. The PCAL suspensions are used\nL58@P3: to calibrate the instrument response with respect to analyte concentration.\nL59@P3: 3.7 Quality Control Sample (QCS) -- A solution of the method analyte of known\nL60@P3: concentrations that is used to fortify an aliquot of LRB matrix. The QCS is\nL61@P3: obtained from a source external to the laboratory, and is used to check\nL62@P3: laboratory performance.\nL63@P3: 3.8 Secondary Calibration Standards (SCAL) -- Commercially prepared, stabilized\nL64@P3: sealed liquid or gel turbidity standards calibrated against properly prepared\nL65@P3: and diluted formazin or styrene divinylbenzene polymers.\nL66@P3: 3.9 Stock Standard Suspension (SSS) -- A concentrated suspension containing the\nL67@P3: analyte prepared in the laboratory using assayed reference materials or\nL68@P3: purchased from a reputable commercial source. Stock standard suspension is\nL69@P3: used to prepare calibration suspensions and other needed suspensions.\nL70@P3: 4.0 INTERFERENCES\nL71@P3: 4.1 The presence of floating debris and coarse sediments which settle out rapidly\nL72@P3: will give low readings. Finely divided air bubbles can cause high readings.\nL73@P3: 4.2 The presence of true color, that is the color of water which is due to dissolved\nL74@P3: substances that absorb light, will cause turbidities to be low, although this\nL75@P3: effect is generally not significant with drinking waters.\nL76@P3: 4.3 Light absorbing materials such as activated carbon in significant concentrations\nL77@P3: can cause low readings.\nL78@P3: 5.0 SAFETY\nL79@P3: 5.1 The toxicity or carcinogenicity of each reagent used in this method has not\nL80@P3: been fully established. Each chemical should be regarded as a potential health\nL81@P3: hazard and exposure should be as low as reasonably achievable.\nL82@P3: 5.2 Each laboratory is responsible for maintaining a current awareness file of\nL83@P3-4: OSHA regulations regarding the safe handling of the chemicals specified in180.1-4\nL84@P4: this method. A reference file of Material Safety Data Sheets (MSDS) should be\nL85@P4: made available to all personnel involved in the chemical analysis. The\nL86@P4: preparation of a formal safety plan is also advisable.\nL87@P4: 5.3 Hydrazine Sulfate (Section 7.2.1) is a carcinogen. It is highly toxic and may be\nL88@P4: fatal if inhaled, swallowed, or absorbed through the skin. Formazin can\nL89@P4: contain residual hydrazine sulfate. Proper protection should be employed.\nL90@P4: 6.0 EQUIPMENT AND SUPPLIES\nL91@P4: 6.1 The turbidimeter shall consist of a nephelometer, with light source for\nL92@P4: illuminating the sample, and one or more photo-electric detectors with a\nL93@P4: readout device to indicate the intensity of light scattered at right angles to the\nL94@P4: path of the incident light. The turbidimeter should be designed so that little\nL95@P4: stray light reaches the detector in the absence of turbidity and should be free\nL96@P4: from significant drift after a short warm-up period.\nL97@P4: 6.2 Differences in physical design of turbidimeters will cause differences in\nL98@P4: measured values for turbidity, even though the same suspension is used for\nL99@P4: calibration. To minimize such differences, the following design criteria should\nL100@P4: be observed:\nL101@P4: 6.2.1 Light source: Tungsten lamp operated at a color temperature between\nL102@P4: 2200-3000°K.\nL103@P4: 6.2.2 Distance traversed by incident light and scattered light within the\nL104@P4: sample tube: Total not to exceed 10 cm.\nL105@P4: 6.2.3 Detector: Centered at 90° to the incident light path and not to exceed\nL106@P4: ±30° from 90°. The detector, and filter system if used, shall have a\nL107@P4: spectral peak response between 400 nm and 600 nm.\nL108@P4: 6.3 The sensitivity of the instrument should permit detection of a turbidity\nL109@P4: difference of 0.02 NTU or less in waters having turbidities less than 1 unit.\nL110@P4: The instrument should measure from 0-40 units turbidity. Several ranges may\nL111@P4: be necessary to obtain both adequate coverage and sufficient sensitivity for low\nL112@P4: turbidities.\nL113@P4: 6.4 The sample tubes to be used with the available instrument must be of clear,\nL114@P4: colorless glass or plastic. They should be kept scrupulously clean, both inside\nL115@P4: and out, and discarded when they become scratched or etched. A light\nL116@P4: coating of silicon oil may be used to mask minor imperfections in glass tubes.\nL117@P4: They must not be handled at all where the light strikes them, but should be\nL118@P4: provided with sufficient extra length, or with a protective case, so that they\nL119@P4: may be handled. Tubes should be checked, indexed and read at the\nL120@P4: orientation that produces the lowest background blank value.\nL121@P4-5: 6.5 Balance -- Analytical, capable of accurately weighing to the nearest 0.0001 g.180.1-5\nL122@P5: 6.6 Glassware -- Class A volumetric flasks and pipets as required.\nL123@P5: 7.0 REAGENTS AND STANDARDS\nL124@P5: 7.1 Reagent water, turbidity-free: Pass deionized distilled water through a 0.45µ\nL125@P5: pore size membrane filter, if such filtered water shows a lower turbidity than\nL126@P5: unfiltered distilled water.\nL127@P5: 7.2 Stock standard suspension (Formazin):\nL128@P5: 7.2.1 Dissolve 1.00 g hydrazine sulfate, (NH22) .H2\nL129@P5: SO 4, (CASRN 10034-93-2) in\nL130@P5: reagent water and dilute to 100 mL in a volumetric flask. CAUTION--\nL131@P5: carcinogen.\nL132@P5: 7.2.2 Dissolve 10.00 g hexamethylenetetramine (CASRN 100-97-0) in reagent\nL133@P5: water and dilute to 100 mL in a volumetric flask. In a 100 mL\nL134@P5: volumetric flask, mix 5.0 mL of each solution (Sections 7.2.1 and 7.2.2).\nL135@P5: Allow to stand 24 hours at 25 ±3°C, then dilute to the mark with\nL136@P5: reagent water.\nL137@P5: 7.3 Primary calibration standards: Mix and dilute 10.00 mL of stock standard\nL138@P5: suspension (Section 7.2) to 100 mL with reagent water. The turbidity of this\nL139@P5: suspension is defined as 40 NTU. For other values, mix and dilute portions of\nL140@P5: this suspension as required.\nL141@P5: 7.3.1 A new stock standard suspension (Section 7.2) should be prepared each\nL142@P5: month. Primary calibration standards (Section 7.3) should be prepared\nL143@P5: daily by dilution of the stock standard suspension.\nL144@P5: 7.4 Formazin in commercially prepared primary concentrated stock standard\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nISO 7027-1:2016 - Water quality — Determination of turbidity — Part 1: Quantitative methods (https://www.iso.org/standard/62801.html)\nciteturn4view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.iso.org/standard/62801.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 178\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn4view5 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.bipm.org/documents/20126/2071204/JCGM_200_2012.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n"
